Benchmark axes:
- Category shape: {linear, linear, sparse dag, dense dag, loops}.
  Most important: Linear, sparse dag. Sparse dag: #edges ~= #nodes.
- Relation args: Member types versus ambient types.
- Member types vs no member types.
- Merges required, no merges required.
  Cases where no merges should be required will probably want to use only preds, no funcs.
- Not fully orthogonal to merges, but we can try: Functions.
  E.g. we might benchmarks where functionality rules only fire trivially (no merges), only once, and requiring recursive merges.
- Rules: None versus member versus ambient.
  For now, focus on member rules.
- Rule interaction with category shape: No parent, single parent, multiple parents.
  Rules that fire on premises inherited from multiple parents versus rules firing on just elements of a single parent versus rules that fire on parent + model owned facts.

In all cases, derive how long a reasonable classical algorithm to compute free models using copy-on-write trees would take.
No long derivation, just the result, double checked by subagents.
Some of these benchmark cases will have more than 1 parameter, e.g. model category size (+ shape) versus relation size.
That's OK, give the bound in terms of multiple variables in those cases.

It's better to duplicate a bit versus trying to make the same program fit multiple use-cases (e.g. some relation is only used in one test case).
This is to make it easy to understand one specific failure versus the whole test suite at once.
Benchmarks should be written in such a way that the input size can be configured.
Each benchmark shoudl be accompanied by a classical rust program using the same cow datastructures that computes the specific free model type directly.
It can compute loops efficiently, traverse the graph efficiently etc.

We should have machinery to validate in each case:
- Does eqlog evaluation have the same scaling (i.e. big O) behavior as the rust program?
- What's the constant: How much faster is the rust program compared to eqlog?

Benchmarks shouldn't take long to run.
Ideally a few hundreds of milliseconds in most cases.
Problem sizes just big enough so that we start observing asymptotic complexity.
If some benchmarks just need to take a few seconds or longer at the moment, they should be ignored by default.

Ideally in each case where the eqlog program already has the expected asymptotic complexity, we should add test cases that make sure that we're not falling behind the rust program.
Not sure how to approach this.
Do we run both, hopefully in a mostly low noise way, and then compare factors?
Hardcode mean, variance, so that we have good confidence intervals (is noise reasonably close to normal though, do we have to remove outliers?).
It's OK to rerun benchmarks a few times to tighten confidence intervals if the benchmarks are fast enough to execute individually.
Maybe we can also add some tools to refresh those ratios.
An improved ratio btw should also trigger a failure, so that we keep those ratios up to date.

For now we only care about performance on this host.
In the future we might also have different expected ratios for different host properties, e.g. cpu family or ram type etc.

Some thoughts for specific cases:
- Measuring just propagation of member data, no rules:
  For member types we only need something like
  ```
  model M {
    type El;
  }
  ```
  The rust host program can then instantiate this with:
  A model category, with each models adding an element, or K elements.

  For predicates of ambient types, add predicates to M on ambient types.
  Similarly for member types (both the model above + predicates on them) and mixed member and ambient elements.
- Measure member rule evaluation from single parent.
  ```
  model M {
    type El;
    pred should_mark(El);
    pred marked(El);

    rule {
      should_mark(el);
      marked(el);
    }
  }
  ```
  Then the rust program should seed every M with an El e and make it should_mark(e) hold.
- Measure member function propagation.
  ```
  model M {
    type El;
    func f(El) -> El;
  }
  ```
  Models could be seeded each with elements e0, e1 such that f(e0) = e1.
  Or f(e) = e.
  No actual element unification at this point.
  Also perhaps from_ambient(AmbientEl) -> El, and seed AmbientEl outer_el with one element per model m, make m.from_ambient(outer_el) defined for each model.
  And the reverse, to_ambient(El) -> AmbientEl, seed each model with its own element e and make m.to_ambient(e) defined.
- Non-recursive congruence applications.
  Same as before with from_ambient case, but give some models the same ambient els, leading to non-trivial congruence applications (since initially, after seeding, each model has its own local El as image under from_ambient).
- Recurisve congruence.
  Same as before, but also use the member function f(El) -> El to produce downstream merges.
  Can even add to_ambient in addition to prouce also some merges outside the model.
- In general it's tricky to make rules fire based on derived data.
  One option is to introduce global AmbientEl type as above and add a member function to_ambient: El -> AmbientEl.
	Then make rules fire on elements that map to some specific AmbientEl, e.g. the same one or such that soem relation among ambient els holds etc.
	However, this machinery itself introduces overhead.
	So before working on benchmarks that require this, we need to make sure that to_ambient member function is represented well, using copy on write efficiently.
